{"name":"Laser Trap","hex":"#ff3f6a","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"laser","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A device that produces a monochromatic, coherent beam of light.","A beam of light produced by such a device; a laser beam."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To cut, destroy or treat with a laser.","To throw or kick with laser-like precision."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/laser"},{"term":"trap","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A machine or other device designed to catch (and sometimes kill) animals, either by holding them in a container, or by catching hold of part of the body.","A trick or arrangement designed to catch someone in a more general sense; a snare."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To physically capture, to catch in a trap or traps, or something like a trap.","To ensnare; to take by stratagem; to entrap."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/trap"}],"entries":[{"title":"Optical tweezers","blocks":[{"text":"Optical tweezers are scientific instruments that use a highly focused laser beam to hold and move microscopic and sub-microscopic objects like atoms, nanoparticles and droplets, in a manner similar to tweezers. If the object is held in air or vacuum without additional support, it can be called optical levitation."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Silica_Nanosphere_in_Optical_Tweezer.jpg/330px-Silica_Nanosphere_in_Optical_Tweezer.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Optical_tweezers","wikidata":"Q1066633"},{"title":"Laser","blocks":[{"text":"A laser is a device that emits light through a process of optical amplification based on the stimulated emission of electromagnetic radiation. The word laser originated as an acronym for light amplification by stimulated emission of radiation. The first laser was built in 1960 by Theodore Maiman at Hughes Research Laboratories, based on theoretical work by Charles H. Townes and Arthur Leonard Schawlow and the optical amplifier patented by Gordon Gould."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/Guiding_the_Milky_Way_%28potw2222a%29.jpg/330px-Guiding_the_Milky_Way_%28potw2222a%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Laser","wikidata":"Q38867"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/61/Optical_cell_rotator.png/500px-Optical_cell_rotator.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/61/Optical_cell_rotator.png/500px-Optical_cell_rotator.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Optical Cell Rotator is a fiber based laser trap that can hold and precisely orient living cells for tomographic microscopy.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AOptical_cell_rotator.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/Green_Laser.jpg/500px-Green_Laser.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/Green_Laser.jpg/1280px-Green_Laser.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/Green_Laser.jpg/500px-Green_Laser.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A laser can produce a very narrow beam of light of a single wavelength, in this case, green.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGreen_Laser.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/Laser_DSC09088.JPG/500px-Laser_DSC09088.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/Laser_DSC09088.JPG/1280px-Laser_DSC09088.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/Laser_DSC09088.JPG/500px-Laser_DSC09088.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A helium–neon laser demonstration. The glow running through the center of the tube is an electric discharge. This glowing plasma is the gain medium for the laser. The laser produces a tiny, intense spot on the screen to the right. The center of the spot appears white because the image is overexposed there.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALaser_DSC09088.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Lasers.JPG/500px-Lasers.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Lasers.JPG/1280px-Lasers.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Lasers.JPG/500px-Lasers.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Red (660 & 635 nm), green (532 & 520 nm), and blue-violet (445 & 405 nm) lasers","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALasers.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/85/Moon_clementine_lidar.jpg/500px-Moon_clementine_lidar.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/85/Moon_clementine_lidar.jpg/1280px-Moon_clementine_lidar.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/85/Moon_clementine_lidar.jpg/500px-Moon_clementine_lidar.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Lidar measurements of lunar topography made by Clementine mission","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMoon_clemen